# Block Ownership Model, Phase 2 (Fork A): the new IR shape a block/jump
# engine runs on, and the smallest possible accessors for it -- see
# docs/plans/block-ownership-model.md section 3.1-3.2 and section 6, Fork A.
#
# IR shape:
#   ["BlockProgramIR", entry_block_name, [blocks]]
#   ["BlockIR", name, [params], [instrs]]
#
# Deliberately NOT today's ["FuncIR", name, params, instrs, lines] shape
# (self_hosting/lib/lower.patlang) even though it looks similar -- no
# `lines` table yet (debugger parity is Phase 8's own job, not this one),
# and semantically a BlockIR is entered by a JUMP that populates exactly
# its own params from a caller-supplied pointer, never a CALL that pushes a
# stack frame. There is no call stack in this engine at all (Fork A,
# "turtles all the way down") -- see interp.patlang's own header for what
# that means for how a program's control flow actually threads through.
#
# Instructions this phase's restricted subset actually emits (a small,
# named subset of what the real lower.patlang supports, not a
# reinterpretation of any of them):
#   ["Const", "num"|"str"|"bool", text]
#   ["Load", name]              ["Store", name]
#   ["Bin", op]                 ["Un", op]
#   ["Jump", n]                 ["JumpIfFalse", n]   -- WITHIN one block only
#   ["Print"]                   -- pops and prints one value; this phase's
#                                  only "host call", standing in for a full
#                                  CallHost dispatch (out of scope here --
#                                  see lower.patlang's own header)
#   ["JumpBlock", target_name, [param_names]]
#                                -- pops one value per param_name (in that
#                                   order), builds a FRESH pointer holding
#                                   only those name->value pairs, and
#                                   transfers control to target_name's own
#                                   BlockIR at instruction 0. Nothing from
#                                   the current block's own locals survives
#                                   this jump except what's explicitly
#                                   listed -- interp.patlang enforces this
#                                   by construction, not by convention (see
#                                   its own header).

make a function called bi_block_name takes blk returns name
  return blk[1]
end

make a function called bi_block_params takes blk returns params
  return blk[2]
end

make a function called bi_block_instrs takes blk returns instrs
  return blk[3]
end

make a function called bi_program_entry takes prog returns name
  return prog[1]
end

make a function called bi_program_blocks takes prog returns blocks
  return prog[2]
end

make a function called bi_find_block takes prog, name returns blk
  let blocks = bi_program_blocks(prog)
  let n = list_len(blocks)
  let i = 0
  while i < n do
    let blk = list_get(blocks, i)
    if bi_block_name(blk) == name then
      return blk
    end
    let i = i + 1
  end
  return false
end
